00:03:52.215 --> 00:03:55.693
about social distancing and
other public health measures.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.180
One final thing before we start talking

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.050
about the background to this paper

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.240
is that what I'm talking about today

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.457
is research done on monkeys

00:04:04.457 --> 00:04:07.070
and that deserves acknowledgement.

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:09.210
Whenever scientists work with animals,

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.380
we think about three big things,

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.450
what we call the three Rs.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:16.180
The principle's to reduce
the number of animals

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:17.470
used in our experiments,

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:20.770
refine our experiments
to maximize the wellbeing

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:22.440
of the animals in the experiments

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.440
and wherever we can replace
animals with other systems,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.580
be they computational systems

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.800
or other systems like
cells in a Petri dish,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.610
and also to replace more
sophisticated animals

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.100
with less sophisticated animals.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:38.750
We'd rather do experiments
on flies than on monkeys.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:40.760
But in some cases, as with this paper,

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.750
it's important that we do this work

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.630
and we need to understand

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.740
as close to relative
to humans as possible.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.670
There are lots of organizations involved

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:51.500
in thinking about these ethical concerns

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.400
and monitoring how we use these organisms.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:55.840
And I've just listed some of them here.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.870
They are, the university
itself, government regulators

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:02.550
and professional organizations,
which certify them.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.820
And this paper comes from a lab

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.580
which is certified these organizations,

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:11.480
and that's the gold standard
in scientific animal care.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.070
Okay, so before we talk about the paper,

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.910
we need to talk a little bit
about how immune memory works.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:19.000
And this is not really news to any of you

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.100
because all of you will know
from your own experience

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.630
that your immune system
remembers past infections

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.519
and stops you getting
sick for a second time.

00:05:27.519 --> 00:05:29.300
What I think of when I think of this

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.930
is how relatively
unconcerned my parents were

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:33.220
when I got chicken pox, right?

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:36.970
Because adults know, who had
chickenpox when they were kids,

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:38.640
that they're really
unlikely to get it again

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:39.473
from their children,

00:05:39.473 --> 00:05:41.480
their immune system has
remembered the virus

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:42.690
that causes chicken pox

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.860
and makes them very unlikely,

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.400
inoculates and protects them

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.830
against getting sick a second time.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:52.440
It's also obviously the
basis of vaccination.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.790
When we vaccinate people,

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.773
what we're doing is we're
introducing a modified,

00:05:58.392 --> 00:06:00.621
weakened strain of a virus or pathogen

00:06:00.621 --> 00:06:03.500
or we're introducing a
fragment of that pathogen

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.380
to cause the immune system to remember it

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.580
so that when you see it
in the flesh for real,

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:12.070
your immune system
prevents you getting sick

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.534
because it's remembered the pathogen

00:06:13.534 --> 00:06:16.803
or remembered the mock
version of the pathogen.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:19.800
So what's going on under the hood here?

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.450
How is your immune system
remembering things?

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.668
Well, it's helpful to break
the immune system down

00:06:24.668 --> 00:06:29.180
into the two branches of it.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:31.850
So we have what's called
innate immunity in humans

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.720
and we have also what's
called adaptive immunity.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.410
And innate immunity is a
general rapid response,

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:41.910
which even starts before you
are infected with a pathogen.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:43.880
It's all the defenses like your skin

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.900
and mucus in your respiratory tract

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.680
that stop you being exposed to
pathogens in the first place

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.740
as well as general defenses
when you get hit by a pathogen.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:57.120
Adaptive immunity is
slower and it's specific

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.420
and it involves the production
of specialized antibodies

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.060
and specialized immune cells.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:04.160
And I'm gonna talk more
about what those immune cells

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:05.900
and antibodies are.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:08.350
But this is the branch
of the immune system,

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:10.350
which is able to remember things.

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.360
And so when we talk about immune memory,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.010
we're talking about the
adaptive immune system

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.510
and we're talking about
a very specific memory

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.443
of a very particular pathogen.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:21.840
So how does it work?

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.390
Well, what happens is a pathogen
such as a virus infects us,

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:28.500
we have a process going on
inside the immune system

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.470
where we learn the
identity of that pathogen.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.860
And then we produce specialized molecules

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.420
known as antibodies that proteins

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:39.230
and we produce specialized
cells which make those proteins

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:42.010
and do other things to
help us become immune.

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:47.010
And those molecules and
those proteins are present,

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:48.890
and help us clear the infection

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:50.870
and they also are present going forward

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:55.870
to help us protect against the
same infection in the future.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:57.670
These are the three key players.

00:07:57.670 --> 00:08:01.070
We've got antibodies, we have
B cells which make antibodies,

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.600
and T-cells, which help B
cells to make antibodies

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:06.450
and do a whole load of other things.

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.640
And obviously many a PhD thesis

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:10.640
has been written about
any one of these things.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.390
So I can't really take you
through them in detail,

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:15.610
but I just want to give
you an overall outline

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.370
of what's going on.

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:19.910
So antibodies work by being sticky

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:22.520
and I've highlighted
the bits of the antibody

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.908
which are sticky, I've just
circled them in white here.

00:08:25.908 --> 00:08:28.760
And really you can think about
them working in in two ways,

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:29.640
they work in lots of ways,

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.420
but two really important ways they work

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:34.687
is by sticking to a virus or
a pathogen inside your body

00:08:34.687 --> 00:08:37.327
and sort of waving to the
rest of the immune system,

00:08:37.327 --> 00:08:40.537
"over here, over here,
I've stuck the bugger.

00:08:40.537 --> 00:08:41.937
"You can come and get him. "

00:08:43.139 --> 00:08:45.510
And they also work by sort
of gumming things up, right?

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.300
If you're a virus and you get
stuck all over by an antibody,

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:49.870
you can imagine it's not really very easy

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:50.880
for you to move around,

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:51.980
for you to do what you wanna do

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:54.500
to interact with the players
you need to interact with

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:57.470
inside the body to cause an infection.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.639
And B cells are obviously
gonna make the antibodies

00:08:59.639 --> 00:09:02.220
and T cells are gonna
support them in that role.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.930
And they are also very specific.

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:07.270
The bits I've circled on
the T cell and the B cell,

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:08.920
like the antibody, are sticky

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:11.970
and they're sticky only in a specific way.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:15.210
So an antibody that's sticky
against the measles virus

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:18.263
will not be sticky against
a different virus like flu.

00:09:18.263 --> 00:09:22.242
And that's really important,
it's a specific response.

00:09:22.242 --> 00:09:25.200
So what happens when
you remember something?

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.980
Well, we can think of it
a little bit like this,

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:31.320
so along the bottom here, I'm
just plotting advancing time.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:34.860
And initially what
happens in my example here

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.560
is you get infected on the
left, there's a blue arrow.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:42.060
And your immune system takes
some time to respond to that,

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:44.160
your adaptive immune system.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.620
You could think of it as it's
finding the right antibody,

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.130
it's calibrating a response.

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:51.000
It's figuring out the best
way to clear this virus,

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.780
this pathogen away.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:54.500
And so this is time that elapses

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.640
and during that time you're
feeling unwell, right?

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.570
Because the pathogen is
running wild, is infecting you,

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:02.250
and that's what being sick is.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:04.190
So then you make your antibodies,

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:06.150
you make your B cells,
you make your T cells

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:08.810
and those help you clear
the infection away.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:13.610
And then subsequently if you
get reinfected with the virus,

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:17.050
your immune system reuses the same cells

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.510
and the same antibodies
it made the first time.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:21.580
There's no point reinventing the wheel,

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:23.330
it's already done this before.

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:25.910
It knows how to get rid of
this particular pathogen,

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:28.360
the correct antibodies,
the correct B cells,

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.230
the correct T cells to use.

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:33.240
And as a result, it's able
to deploy them very quickly

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.288
to help clear the infection away.

00:10:35.288 --> 00:10:37.523
But your immune system is
actually even clever than that

00:10:37.523 --> 00:10:40.570
because it doesn't just
use the same things again,

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:44.130
it uses the same things
again in a more strong

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.710
and extensive way.

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:48.670
So if we plot, again here,

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.620
I'm plotting time along the bottom here

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.710
and I'm plotting going up the side,

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:54.910
the strength of response.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:57.890
After the first infection
you have a weaker response

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:01.530
that occurs after a larger period of time.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:03.110
Whereas after the second exposure,

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:04.950
you have a stronger response

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:07.350
that occurs after a
shorter period of time.

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.750
So your immune system is reusing the cells

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:12.590
and the molecules it
generated the first time

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:14.170
and it's deploying them harder

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:16.970
and faster to help you beat
the pathogen more quickly

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:19.870
and prevent you going through
that period of getting sick.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:22.990
Okay, so can I bring this to life?

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:25.290
How can I make this a
bit clearer to you guys?

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:28.340
Well, I have an example or
something of an illustration.

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:31.080
So if you'll indulge me for a moment,

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:32.573
I have a problem for you.

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:35.350
and the problem is this;

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:36.770
Maria went to the bread store

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.190
to buy a loaf of bread for dinner.

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:41.003
She had two quarters, four dimes,

00:11:41.003 --> 00:11:42.791
three nickels and two pennies.

00:11:42.791 --> 00:11:46.550
And the total cost of
the bread with 82 cents.

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:47.580
She promised to make sure

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:50.670
she had exactly one coin
remaining after the purchase.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:52.770
So the question I'm setting for you is

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:55.300
which coin did she have left
after buying the loaf of bread?

