00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:46.663
And that's a little bit

00:13:46.663 --> 00:13:48.810
like what your immune system is doing.

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:50.850
So I sent you this math problem

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:52.780
and I give you this period
of time to work it out,

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:54.803
you have to get your calculator out,

00:13:56.298 --> 00:13:58.680
pull your solution together
and then you answer me.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.200
And then I ask you the
same question a bit later

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:04.100
and you don't need time
to work it out again

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:04.990
'cause you just remember,

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:07.210
I've already done this,
the answer is a quarter.

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:08.309
What are you doing, Joe?

00:14:08.309 --> 00:14:11.710
So that's what your immune
system does in a way.

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:13.620
It has it's first infection,

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:15.830
and it's spending this time figuring out

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:17.970
how to fight the infection.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:19.990
And then it comes up with a good response

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:21.420
with a specific antibody

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.900
that will target that
particular infection.

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:26.070
And then if you re-exposed
it the second time

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:28.830
to the same pathogenic again,

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:31.470
it's not gonna rework out
how to fight that pathogen.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:34.140
It's just gonna reuse
it's solution from before

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:36.690
and deploy that response harder and faster

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:40.240
as we saw before, and
prevent you from getting sick

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:42.190
and clear the infection really quickly.

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:46.490
Okay, so we've said
that your immune system

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:48.245
remembers past infections
to stop you getting sick

00:14:48.245 --> 00:14:49.330
for a second time.

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:52.090
And we said that it's
the basis of vaccination.

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:54.840
But memory is a funny thing.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.220
And I'm thinking about my
own memory when I say that.

00:14:58.220 --> 00:15:01.310
Because we remember some
things very, very well,

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:05.740
perfectly even though they
occurred a very long time ago.

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:10.000
So this is me on my first
day of secondary school

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.090
with my two brothers.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:15.563
I think one of them is on, so
thank you for joining Zack.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:20.350
But I'm the one on the left,
you can tell I have dimples,

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:21.730
the other two do not.

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:23.060
But the point of putting this here

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:24.950
is I remember this day really well.

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:27.380
I remember how I felt, I
remember feeling nervous,

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:29.250
I remember meeting my
teacher for the first time,

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:30.450
the walk to school,

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:33.310
meeting people who are
gonna become my friends.

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:36.400
And it viscerally quite
burned into my brain.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:38.150
This is not a day I'm gonna forget.

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:41.240
On the other hand, there are some things

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:42.700
that we do very often

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:45.023
that we don't remember
all that well at all.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.040
And so here is a picture from my phone

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:51.110
of the instructions as
to how to open my mailbox

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:53.110
at the university,

00:15:53.110 --> 00:15:54.417
turn left a certain number of times

00:15:54.417 --> 00:15:56.010
and then write to set the number of times,

00:15:56.010 --> 00:15:57.447
it's actually a very
difficult thing to do.

00:15:57.447 --> 00:16:00.530
But the point is, I've done
this dozens and dozens of times

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:02.010
and I don't remember it.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:03.860
Every time I wanna open my mailbox,

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:05.860
I pull out my phone and
I look at the picture

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:08.933
because I don't remember how
to do it without that prompt.

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:12.660
So your immune system isn't
very different than this,

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:15.080
in the sense that it can
remember some things very well

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:17.260
and other things much less well.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:22.260
So the question really is is
COVID-19 like my mailbox code

00:16:22.311 --> 00:16:24.260
or is it like my first day of school?

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:27.020
Is it something that your immune
system remembers very well

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:27.853
or is it something

00:16:27.853 --> 00:16:30.772
that your immune system
remembers really poorly,

00:16:30.772 --> 00:16:32.250
or is it somewhere in between?

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:35.210
That's not a binary, there's
a continuum of options

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:38.170
between being remembered
really well or not really well.

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:39.680
And if we look at other pathogens,

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.800
we can see that your
immune system is exhibiting

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:44.700
this continuum of memory.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:46.940
So a pathogen like measles,

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:49.700
the measles virus is actually
extremely well remembered

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:51.290
by your immune system.

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:54.170
So if you get infected with measles,

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:56.150
your immune system will
generate a response

00:16:56.150 --> 00:17:00.970
and that response, that specific
response will be remembered

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:02.820
for decades and decades.

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:05.090
And it's why measles
is a very good disease

00:17:05.090 --> 00:17:06.760
to vaccinate people against.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:08.661
You vaccinate them as children

00:17:08.661 --> 00:17:12.410
and they have immunity lasting
for decades and decades

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:14.540
going forward from that point.

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:16.640
And in fact, if you ask an immunologist

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:18.490
to estimate how long the memory,

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:20.932
the immune memory of measles lasts,

00:17:20.932 --> 00:17:23.840
they might tell you up to 200 years.

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:26.180
So this is an extremely good memory

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:28.900
that your immune system
generates of measles

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:30.150
and it's why you get vaccinated

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:31.910
only at the beginning of your childhood

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:33.520
and you don't need to see the virus again

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:34.570
to be immune from it.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:40.410
Tetanus is a bacteria that
causes infections in people.

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:44.050
And many of you may have
had multiple tetanus shots

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:44.883
over your life.

00:17:44.883 --> 00:17:47.680
In fact, it's quite common
to have tetanus boosters

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:48.890
every 10 years or so.

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:50.030
Because whilst your immune system

00:17:50.030 --> 00:17:51.910
is pretty good at remembering tetanus,

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:53.580
it does need periodic reminders

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:55.880
and that's what those boosters serve as,

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.380
to prevent you forgetting tetanus.

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:01.810
So tetanus, we could put
somewhere in between a measles

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:05.220
and the last disease I'm gonna talk about,

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:07.140
which is just the common cold.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:08.720
Now I should say that the common cold

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:10.800
is actually quite a complicated collection

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:12.270
of different diseases.

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:15.490
It's actually caused by
many different viruses.

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:16.630
But each one of those viruses

00:18:16.630 --> 00:18:20.053
is actually pretty poorly
remembered by your immune system.

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:22.276
And that's not that surprising

00:18:22.276 --> 00:18:26.220
because we know from
experience that we get colds

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:28.100
year after year after year after year.

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:31.320
It's not like being a
senior citizen protects you

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:32.580
against getting a cold.

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:35.090
Unfortunately, people
throughout their entire life

00:18:35.090 --> 00:18:36.440
get sick with colds.

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:38.010
And that's because your immune system

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:39.770
is not remembering this pathogen.

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:42.730
So it can't reuse the
response it used last time,

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:44.910
it has to start from scratch every time.

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:46.960
And as a result, you get sick.

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:48.360
Because you have that delayed period

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:49.820
where your immune system is figuring out

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:52.530
how to get rid of the disease.

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:55.650
So where does COVID-19
fit on this continuum?

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:58.080
Is it closer to the common cold end,

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:00.240
the measles end or
somewhere in the middle?

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:01.310
And that's really the problem

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:04.370
that these authors in this
paper try to shed some light.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:05.910
Okay, so enough background,

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:09.150
let's dive into actually
what's done in this paper

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:11.210
and what's really going on.

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:13.243
So what do the authors do in this paper?

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:15.913
I'm gonna take you through.

00:19:18.265 --> 00:19:23.265
So what they're doing is they
start off on the left here

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:24.853
with some monkeys,

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:28.940
and what they do is
they initially challenge

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:32.430
or expose those monkeys to SARS-CoV-2

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:34.643
the virus that causes COVID-19.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:38.880
And you can see the days being counted off

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.970
on the bottom here, from
that period on the left.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:43.210
And I'm gonna take you
through the rest of it,

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:44.370
but just you can see the days

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:45.650
and I've given you an indication

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:49.220
that DPI stands for Days
Post Initial Infection

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:51.660
and DPR stands for Days Post reinfection.

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:54.380
That second number will
make more sense in a moment.

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:55.630
So we've initially challenged

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:57.780
or exposed the monkeys to the virus

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:00.260
and then we're gonna monitor
them to see if they get sick,

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:03.450
which they should because we've
exposed them to this virus,

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:05.150
but also make sure that they get better.

00:20:05.150 --> 00:20:08.933
So we want them to experience
the disease and then recover.

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:12.810
Then we're gonna take a
subset of those monkeys

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:14.500
and we're gonna re-challenge them

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:17.800
or re-expose them to the
same virus, SARS-CoV-2.

00:20:18.706 --> 00:20:21.870
And then basically just ask
did the monkeys get sick?

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:23.460
Because if they got sick

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:25.080
then it suggests that their immune system

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:26.400
didn't remember the virus.

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:27.385
And if they didn't get sick

00:20:27.385 --> 00:20:29.480
then it suggests that their immune system

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:31.703
did remember the virus.

00:20:31.703 --> 00:20:34.530
Okay, so that's in broad brush strokes

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:36.470
what the authors have done here.

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:37.980
But how do you do that?

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:40.610
How do you go about
monitoring these things?

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:43.440
What's the matter of
being sick or not sick?

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:44.560
Well, there's a few different measures

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:46.200
that the authors are gonna use.

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:48.050
The first one is swabs,

00:20:48.050 --> 00:20:49.380
and this is gonna be familiar to you

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:52.920
because people all over
the world who are concerned

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:55.940
that they have COVID
are going into hospitals

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:57.130
and getting swabs.

00:20:57.130 --> 00:20:59.610
And then what we do is we look for,

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:03.440
scientists look for the presence
of the virus on that swab.

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:05.180
And that's exactly what
the authors have done here

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:06.158
with the monkeys.

00:21:06.158 --> 00:21:08.530
There's a periodic
sampling of the monkeys,

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:11.190
where they're being swamped
to basically monitor,

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:13.503
to look for, can we
find virus in the monkey

00:21:13.503 --> 00:21:17.020
after we initially infect it,
after we tried to reinfect it,

00:21:17.020 --> 00:21:18.930
and when it's sick and
when it's recovered.

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:20.590
So that's one thing we're gonna do.

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:23.220
Number two, we're going
to look for antibodies.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:25.820
Now, I told you that antibodies
are these sticky proteins

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:29.650
that your body produces in
response to a specific pathogen.

00:21:29.650 --> 00:21:32.480
So here we're gonna be looking
for specific antibodies,

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:35.620
Abs is short for antibodies.

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:40.450
Specific antibodies that
respond to the SARS-CoV-2 virus.

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:41.905
And we're gonna look for them

00:21:41.905 --> 00:21:44.020
after we've challenged
the monkeys initially

00:21:44.020 --> 00:21:46.200
and then after they've been re-challenged

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:48.590
with this infection.

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:51.140
So those are gonna
report on what's going on

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:53.120
in the monkey's blood with antibodies

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:54.970
and what's going on with the virus

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:57.060
but how do we know if the
monkey is actually sick?

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:58.880
Well, we can use the same things

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:00.640
that we would use on people.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:02.280
We can use x-rays.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:04.840
So we can take pictures
of the monkeys' lungs

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:06.320
to see whether they look healthy

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:08.330
or whether they don't look healthy.

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:10.965
And chest x-rays are
gonna be used by doctors,

00:22:10.965 --> 00:22:13.400
they are used by doctors
all over the world

00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:16.260
to look inside your lungs to
see do you have pneumonia?

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:17.960
Is there fluid in your lungs

00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:20.480
which is gonna be obstructing
your ability to breathe

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.150
and get oxygen from the air

00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:25.580
and breathe out carbon dioxide
that your body's producing?

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:28.520
So we're gonna take them when
the monkey should be healthy,

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:30.060
when we think it might be sick,

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:31.420
and then when we're interested to see

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:32.670
whether it's sick or healthy,

00:22:32.670 --> 00:22:33.818
because we want to see

00:22:33.818 --> 00:22:37.710
if it's an immune system
remembered the virus.

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:39.890
And then the last thing we can do

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:42.051
is what's known as histopathology.

00:22:42.051 --> 00:22:43.860
And histopathology basically

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:46.310
just means looking at the monkey's lung

00:22:46.310 --> 00:22:47.767
under the microscope.

00:22:47.767 --> 00:22:52.490
Having a in-detailed look at
the microscopic cellular level,

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:53.323
what's going on?

00:22:53.323 --> 00:22:55.230
Does the monkeys long look healthy

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:56.860
or does it not look healthy?

00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:59.600
Does it look like the
monkey has got the disease

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:01.880
or does it look like the
monkey is in the clear

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:03.080
and it's pretty healthy?

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:05.760
So those are the experiments,

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:08.420
I'm gonna be taking you through,
those four different areas;

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:11.410
looking for swabs of the
virus, looking at antibodies

00:23:11.410 --> 00:23:13.790
to see if the monkeys
mounting an immune response

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:14.623
to the virus,

00:23:14.623 --> 00:23:18.270
looking at x-rays to see
how the monkey's chest looks

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:19.640
and looking at histopathology,

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:21.610
looking at the monkeys
lungs under the microscope

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:23.260
to see whether they look healthy.

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:27.280
Okay, so if that's what they
did, what did they find?

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:28.653
What did the authors show?

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.400
So before I show you the data on swabs,

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.800
I just wanna explain to you
how it's gonna be displayed.

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:36.860
They're gonna be showed on these graphs.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:38.440
I'm sorry about the quality of the graphs,

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:39.760
they're not wonderful.

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:42.310
That's not my fault, it's
the fault of the authors.

00:23:43.270 --> 00:23:46.640
Unfortunately in the published work

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:49.410
the graphs are not fantastic quality.

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:52.790
But along the bottom we're
looking at days post infection,

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:55.580
so the number of days that
have elapsed since exposure

00:23:55.580 --> 00:23:58.760
or re-exposure to the
virus in the case of DPR,

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:01.480
which would be days post reinfection.

00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:02.730
And then along the side,

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:05.293
we're looking at viral
loads in nasal swaps.

00:24:05.293 --> 00:24:07.833
And what that really just
means is the amount of virus

00:24:07.833 --> 00:24:09.680
that we detected on the swabs

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:12.050
that we took from the monkey's nose.

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:13.930
I should point out that,

00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:17.690
along the side is actually
a logarithmic scale.

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:19.793
And you might be familiar with this,

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:22.370
with something like the Richter scale,

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:25.170
which is used to measure
how strong earthquakes are.

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:28.590
Basically what it means
is as you go up the scale,

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:31.540
the distances between
the numbers increases.

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:34.520
So the distance between one on this scale

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:37.840
and two is actually only one
10th as big as the distance

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:41.090
between two and three and so on and so on.

00:24:41.090 --> 00:24:42.550
So really all that means

00:24:42.550 --> 00:24:45.670
is if you move up on a logarithmic scale,

00:24:45.670 --> 00:24:48.210
you're getting a lot
bigger, a lot more quickly.

00:24:48.210 --> 00:24:52.330
It's just a way that we
use of displaying data

00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:55.513
so that really big points don't
block out really small ones.

00:24:56.960 --> 00:24:57.793
Cool.

00:24:57.793 --> 00:24:59.790
Okay, so that's taking through the graph.

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:01.520
So let's look at the graphs.

00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:04.960
So I'm splitting the monkeys
here into two groups.

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:07.340
Monkeys zero, one and two,

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:08.740
and I'm displaying the data

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:11.680
about their swabs on the bottom left

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:14.030
and then the other group is
monkeys three, four, five

00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:16.920
and six which I'm
displaying in the middle.

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:18.337
And the monkeys zero, one and two

00:25:18.337 --> 00:25:20.830
are the monkeys we just infected once.

00:25:20.830 --> 00:25:23.420
So these monkeys are not
going to be reinfected

00:25:23.420 --> 00:25:25.110
whereas monkey three, four, five and six,

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:27.100
we're going to attempt to reinfect them,

00:25:27.100 --> 00:25:28.710
we're gonna see what happens.

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:32.750
So in both cases, after we
initially infect the monkeys,

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:35.230
at day zero, you can see
we don't detect virus

00:25:35.230 --> 00:25:36.940
in the nose of the monkeys.

00:25:36.940 --> 00:25:38.390
And then unsurprisingly,

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:40.277
we see this big increase
in the amount of virus

00:25:40.277 --> 00:25:43.943
that's being detected in the
swabs from the monkey's nose.

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:47.110
That's totally consistent
with the monkeys getting sick,

00:25:47.110 --> 00:25:48.710
the virus is replicating

00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:50.590
inside the monkeys respiratory tract,

00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:53.520
and you can pick it up
in swabs of the nose.

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:55.040
And then also on a surprisingly,

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:59.450
the virus is reduced around
day 10 post infection.

00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:00.577
You can see that the monkey

00:26:00.577 --> 00:26:04.100
has successfully fought the
virus off, it looks like, right?

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:05.870
'Cause we're not detecting virus

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:06.840
in the monkey's nose anymore.

00:26:06.840 --> 00:26:10.740
It looks like all the virus
has been killed by the monkey.

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:11.840
So what's gonna happen

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:14.543
when we attempt to reinfect the monkey?

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:17.290
Well, let's have a look.

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:20.020
So at 28 days post reinfection,

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:21.540
if you look at the middle here,

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:23.390
that dotted line represents the point

00:26:23.390 --> 00:26:26.297
at which we re-exposed
the monkeys to the virus.

00:26:26.297 --> 00:26:28.730
And what you can see, quite remarkably,

00:26:28.730 --> 00:26:31.040
is that there's absolutely no increase

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:33.640
in the amount of virus
detected in these monkeys,

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:35.010
monkeys, three, four, five and six,

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:36.840
that is after the re-exposure.

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:38.570
And that's in stark contrast

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:39.403
to what happened

00:26:39.403 --> 00:26:41.690
the first time we exposed
the monkeys to the virus

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:42.901
where you saw this big spike

00:26:42.901 --> 00:26:45.533
in the amount of virus that was detected.

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:49.000
Now on the right here,
what I'm showing you

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:54.000
is the comparisons of the reinfection,

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:56.247
amount of virus detected

00:26:56.247 --> 00:26:59.140
and the initial infection
amount of virus detected

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:00.807
at specific days.

00:27:00.807 --> 00:27:04.040
So, you'll see the first
two bars refer to day zero,

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:07.150
the second two to day
five, day seven, day 14,

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:09.800
post either infection or reinfection.

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:12.127
And the blue dot represents the bar,

00:27:12.127 --> 00:27:15.361
which is in the initial
infection situation

00:27:15.361 --> 00:27:18.160
and the orange dot represents the bar

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:20.363
in the reinfection situation.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:23.540
Now, the reason in the first
two you can't see any bars

00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:25.951
is because they didn't detect
any virus at this point.

00:27:25.951 --> 00:27:29.420
And then at day five, you
can see under the blue dot,

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:33.370
there is virus detected in the
initial infection situation.

00:27:33.370 --> 00:27:36.410
But in the reinfection situation,
there's no virus detected.

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:39.450
Same as to at day seven and
day 14, there's no difference

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:42.000
because under both situations

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:43.640
there's no virus detected at this point.

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:45.890
The monkeys who were initially infected,

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:48.500
by day 14 have successfully
cleared the virus

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:51.530
out of their system and
so you can't find it.

00:27:51.530 --> 00:27:55.500
So interestingly, this is also replicated,

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:57.930
I say interestingly, it's
pretty unsurprising actually,

00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:00.900
it's also replicated if you
swab the monkey in other places.

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:02.514
If you take a swab from
the monkey's throat

00:28:02.514 --> 00:28:04.440
or a swab from the monkey's anus,

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:07.490
both of which are places that
we'd expect to find virus

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:09.240
during an active infection.

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:11.370
And you can see that what's happening here

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:13.328
is the monkey initially,

00:28:13.328 --> 00:28:17.890
will see a spike in a virus
detected by the swabs,

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:20.410
but when we reinfect them
in the central two panels,

00:28:20.410 --> 00:28:23.750
you can see that there's no virus detected

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:25.280
after we reinfect the monkey

00:28:26.241 --> 00:28:28.350
or we attempt to reinfect the monkey.

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:30.751
So collectively I think this
should give you a picture

00:28:30.751 --> 00:28:32.780
that after we initially infect the monkey,

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:36.030
it looks like there's virus being produced

00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:39.000
and we can pick it up in
swabs of the monkey's nose

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:40.380
and the monkey's throat.

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:42.410
But if we allow the monkey to recover

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:46.010
and then re-expose it 28 days
after the initial exposure,

00:28:46.010 --> 00:28:47.930
we can't see virus replicating,

00:28:47.930 --> 00:28:49.070
which suggests that the monkey

00:28:49.070 --> 00:28:51.410
is successfully fighting the virus off

00:28:51.410 --> 00:28:53.560
and preventing it from replicating

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:55.768
and establishing an infection.

00:28:55.768 --> 00:28:57.070
Cool.

00:28:57.070 --> 00:29:00.140
So we've talked about the
first one of my four measures.

00:29:00.140 --> 00:29:02.050
Now let's talk about the second one,

00:29:02.050 --> 00:29:03.733
which is looking at antibodies.

00:29:04.920 --> 00:29:07.330
Okay, so we're looking at antibodies

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:09.360
at various different points

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:12.100
along the progression of this experiment.

00:29:12.100 --> 00:29:14.460
And what you can see here is again,

00:29:14.460 --> 00:29:16.590
I'm talking about monkey
zero, one and two.

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:19.020
These are the monkeys that
were infected only once

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:21.060
at the beginning, at day zero.

00:29:21.060 --> 00:29:25.270
And you can see that, excuse
me, as the experiment moves on,

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:27.110
there is this little lag

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:29.120
where the monkeys are trying
to generate the antibodies.

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:32.720
That's the gap in the plots
I was showing you earlier,

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:35.330
the monkey's immune system is
looking for the right antibody

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:36.890
to fight this virus off.

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:39.130
Looking, looking, looking,
searching, it finds it,

00:29:39.130 --> 00:29:42.670
and then you can see it produced,
beginning around day 14,

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:44.657
and then into date, 21 and 28.

00:29:44.657 --> 00:29:47.240
And you can see relatively high levels

00:29:47.240 --> 00:29:49.170
of the antibodies present.

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:52.170
So what about in the situation
where we reinfect the monkey?

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:57.170
Well, what you can see is
the first half of this graph

00:29:57.170 --> 00:29:59.020
is basically the same.

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:00.670
We initially infected the monkey,

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:02.580
you can see it's looking
for the antibody looking,

00:30:02.580 --> 00:30:04.800
looking, looking, looking,
it finds it around day 14,

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:06.930
we get production of the antibody.

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:10.430
And then around day 28 when
we reinfect these monkeys,

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:13.690
you can see that they then
kick into a higher gear.

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:15.690
They're producing even more antibodies

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:17.080
and there's this enormous peak

00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:19.263
at day 42 post initial infection,

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:23.510
which is 14 days after
the second infection.

00:30:23.510 --> 00:30:25.990
And the antibody levels at
that point are much higher

00:30:25.990 --> 00:30:29.220
than they were 14 days
after the first infection.

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:31.450
And that's what's
spotted on the far right,

00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:34.420
you can see 14 days
post initial infection,

00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:36.580
you've got some antibody produced,

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:39.650
but 14 days post reinfection,

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:42.030
you have even more antibody produced.

00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:43.710
Which suggests, I mean, that backs up

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:44.760
what I was telling you earlier

00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:47.430
about the idea of the immune
response kicking in quicker

00:30:47.430 --> 00:30:49.730
and more punchy, it's being more effective

00:30:49.730 --> 00:30:51.930
and more strong the second time around

00:30:51.930 --> 00:30:54.250
and that's stopping the
monkey getting sick.

00:30:54.250 --> 00:30:56.390
And so for these monkeys don't look like

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:57.750
in terms of the antibodies,

00:30:57.750 --> 00:30:58.700
they look like they've mounted

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:01.020
a really effective second
response the second time

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:02.250
you've exposed them

00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:05.440
and it looks like that is the basis maybe

00:31:05.440 --> 00:31:07.040
of why they're not getting sick.

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:10.726
But so far we haven't
actually taken a look

00:31:10.726 --> 00:31:13.230
at what's happening in the monkeys' lungs.

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:14.373
So let's now do that.

00:31:15.350 --> 00:31:17.420
So let's look at some x-ray pictures.

00:31:17.420 --> 00:31:20.450
We're gonna look at it first
an x-ray picture from day zero.

00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:24.090
This is taken before we expose the monkeys

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:25.840
to the virus in the first place.

00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:28.860
So this should be a healthy monkey lung.

00:31:28.860 --> 00:31:30.790
And you can see,
hopefully down the middle,

00:31:30.790 --> 00:31:32.140
you can see the spine of the monkey,

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:34.640
you can see the ribs
coming in from either side

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:37.580
and you can see the monkey's heart

00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:40.090
and major blood vessels in the middle.

00:31:40.090 --> 00:31:42.230
Now, on x-rays, first of all,

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:44.280
I should say I'm not a radiologist

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:46.650
and people train for a
very long period of time

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:47.750
to look at these x-rays

00:31:47.750 --> 00:31:49.776
and be able to interpret them properly.

00:31:49.776 --> 00:31:53.320
But what I do know is x-rays of the lungs

00:31:53.320 --> 00:31:56.130
should show up in dark
if there's lots of air.

00:31:56.130 --> 00:31:58.020
Because it means that the x-rays move

00:31:58.020 --> 00:31:59.240
through the lungs more easily

00:31:59.240 --> 00:32:02.890
and so that shows up as a
black color on the x-ray.

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:05.240
And so it's good to have
lots of darker color

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:06.370
on your chest x-ray,

00:32:06.370 --> 00:32:09.410
because it means that
your lungs are full of air

00:32:09.410 --> 00:32:11.303
and that's what they should be full of.

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:13.560
Unfortunately,

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:16.240
it looks like after seven
days post infection,

00:32:16.240 --> 00:32:17.550
if you strain your eyes

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:19.930
and look very closely or
if you're a radiologist,

00:32:19.930 --> 00:32:22.300
I'm told, you can see there's beginning

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:23.710
to look like some cloudiness.

00:32:23.710 --> 00:32:26.340
And that's particularly
pronounced in the red circle

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:30.410
that the authors have kindly
drawn on this x-ray for us.

00:32:30.410 --> 00:32:33.923
Now physicians refer to these
as a ground-glass opacit.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:38.490
And really what they are is
it's the production of fluid

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:39.487
and inflammation in the lung,

00:32:39.487 --> 00:32:42.850
which is basically replacing
the air in the lung

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:46.030
and causing the chest
x-ray to look lighter

00:32:46.030 --> 00:32:48.860
rather than darker because
it's not moving through air.

00:32:48.860 --> 00:32:52.290
Okay, so that's what's happening
when the monkey's sick,

00:32:52.290 --> 00:32:54.410
that suggests that the
monkey's developing pneumonia

00:32:54.410 --> 00:32:55.243
when it's sick.

00:32:55.243 --> 00:32:56.440
Again, that's not surprising,

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:59.270
that's what happens with
a respiratory infection

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:01.715
like SARS-CoV-2.

00:33:01.715 --> 00:33:03.003
So let's look at what happens

00:33:03.003 --> 00:33:05.860
when the monkey gets better at day 28,

00:33:05.860 --> 00:33:08.190
immediately before we re-challenge it

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:10.920
with a new specimen of the virus.

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:13.950
So what you can see here is
in the blue shaded circle,

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:16.310
the lung looks clearer here, doesn't it?

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:18.199
So we can see less cloudiness,

00:33:18.199 --> 00:33:21.830
it looks like there's no
fluid appearing in the lungs,

00:33:21.830 --> 00:33:24.190
suggesting the monkey has
got rid of the pneumonia

00:33:24.190 --> 00:33:25.330
it may be had

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:28.600
and it's on the way to
being healthy again.

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:31.300
And then if we look after we reinfect it,

00:33:31.300 --> 00:33:33.500
five days after we reinfect the monkey,

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:36.140
that clearness remains on the chest x-ray,

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:38.750
suggesting that monkey
did not develop pneumonia

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:40.400
the second time around.

00:33:40.400 --> 00:33:41.940
So the picture we're getting from x-rays

00:33:41.940 --> 00:33:43.630
is we initially infected the monkey,

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:45.530
its lungs got filled up with fluid

00:33:45.530 --> 00:33:47.591
and it got pneumonia, it recovered.

00:33:47.591 --> 00:33:49.630
And when we reinfected the monkey,

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:52.170
it doesn't look like
that pneumonia developed.

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:55.090
It looks like the monkey's lung
remained relatively healthy

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:57.490
and clear and that suggests
it didn't get disease,

00:33:57.490 --> 00:34:00.320
it wasn't really sick
the second time around.

00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:03.440
So finally, let's have a look
at the monkey's lung itself

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:04.653
under the microscope.

00:34:06.010 --> 00:34:07.480
So what I'm showing you here

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:11.320
are two pictures from the monkey's lung.

00:34:11.320 --> 00:34:13.780
On top is a sort of zoomed-out version

00:34:13.780 --> 00:34:15.570
of the same picture on the bottom

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:16.403
and I've annotated it

00:34:16.403 --> 00:34:19.320
to show you two important things in this.

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:21.670
The first is that there's
a lot of white space

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:23.360
on this picture

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:25.790
and that's because there's
a lot of air in the lungs.

00:34:25.790 --> 00:34:28.120
That's how it should be,
the lungs are full of air.

00:34:28.120 --> 00:34:29.960
They're the organ that does,

00:34:29.960 --> 00:34:32.260
that allows oxygen into the blood stream

00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:34.840
and excrete CO2 out to the body.

00:34:34.840 --> 00:34:36.780
And then we have these thin walls

00:34:36.780 --> 00:34:38.920
around the edge of the air sacs.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:40.170
And you'll have to take my word for it

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:43.410
that those are blood vessels
and they're very thin

00:34:43.410 --> 00:34:44.300
and that's important

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:47.420
because it means that the
oxygen in the air sacs

00:34:47.420 --> 00:34:49.970
can move very easily into the bloodstream

00:34:49.970 --> 00:34:51.500
and the carbon dioxide in the bloodstream

00:34:51.500 --> 00:34:53.770
can move very easily into the air sacs

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:55.970
to be excreted and
breathed out of the body.

00:34:57.540 --> 00:35:01.190
Cool, so what happens when
we look at some monkey lungs

00:35:01.190 --> 00:35:03.990
taken from seven days after
the initial infection?

00:35:03.990 --> 00:35:05.610
What do they look like?

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:07.840
Well, it's not a pretty picture.

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:11.430
We've lost a lot of the clear white space

00:35:11.430 --> 00:35:13.810
that should appear on the micrograph

00:35:13.810 --> 00:35:15.640
and that's indicative of the air sacs

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:16.800
getting much, much smaller

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:19.050
because there's this massive
inflammatory response

00:35:19.050 --> 00:35:20.030
in the lung.

00:35:20.030 --> 00:35:24.759
We've got much, much thicker
walls of the air sacs,

00:35:24.759 --> 00:35:28.260
smaller air sacs and it doesn't
look like a pretty picture.

00:35:28.260 --> 00:35:29.100
It looks like this monkey

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:31.030
is gonna be having trouble breathing,

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:33.680
it's certainly not in a healthy place.

00:35:33.680 --> 00:35:38.680
And if we compare that to
a similar lung looked at,

00:35:38.760 --> 00:35:41.220
but we're now looking after
we reinfected the monkey,

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:43.840
after it's recovered,
and we've reinfected it,

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:45.160
what does it look like?

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:47.180
Well, it's a much, much
better looking lung.

00:35:47.180 --> 00:35:49.730
We've still got nice big air sacks,

00:35:49.730 --> 00:35:51.340
maybe it looks a little bit different,

00:35:51.340 --> 00:35:53.730
it looks a little bit more inflamed

00:35:53.730 --> 00:35:55.040
and gorged than the control,

00:35:55.040 --> 00:35:56.876
but it's certainly a
hell of a lot healthier

00:35:56.876 --> 00:35:58.670
than the lung in the middle.

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:00.877
So if we combine this with the x-ray,

00:36:00.877 --> 00:36:03.410
the picture we're getting is healthy lungs

00:36:03.410 --> 00:36:08.290
at the beginning after
initially infecting the monkey,

00:36:08.290 --> 00:36:11.120
the monkey's lungs
unfortunately become diseased

00:36:11.120 --> 00:36:12.180
and we get inflammation,

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:13.910
the air sacs gets washed out.

00:36:13.910 --> 00:36:17.060
And so this lung isn't working so well.

00:36:17.060 --> 00:36:19.394
We allow the monkey to
recover, we reinfect it

00:36:19.394 --> 00:36:22.220
and the lungs don't look
bad when they're reinfected.

00:36:22.220 --> 00:36:26.350
They look basically normal
and basically quite healthy.

00:36:26.350 --> 00:36:28.160
So collectively then I think the authors

00:36:28.160 --> 00:36:30.090
have pretty convincingly shown

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:32.769
that monkeys do not get
reinfected by SARS-CoV-2

00:36:32.769 --> 00:36:35.410
after a second exposure,

00:36:35.410 --> 00:36:38.080
four weeks after the first infection.

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:40.800
And we know that because
when we exposed the monkeys,

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:42.930
we don't detect virus in swabs

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:44.860
from the monkeys' noses and throats,

00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:47.330
we don't detect pneumonia in the x-rays

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:48.840
taken of the monkeys lungs,

00:36:48.840 --> 00:36:52.740
and the monkeys lungs look
healthy under the microscope.

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:53.573
Cool.

00:36:53.573 --> 00:36:55.950
So that's what the authors have shown.

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:57.970
How does that fit in
with what we already know

00:36:57.970 --> 00:37:00.613
and what questions remain outstanding?

00:37:02.020 --> 00:37:04.030
Well, I think the most important question

00:37:04.030 --> 00:37:06.360
that remains outstanding
is to do with timing.

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:09.260
And this is a really
difficult question to answer.

00:37:09.260 --> 00:37:10.830
It's not that scientists haven't tried

00:37:10.830 --> 00:37:11.710
to answer this question

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:13.670
or don't have good
experiments for addressing it,

00:37:13.670 --> 00:37:16.350
it's just it's very difficult
to do these experiments.

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:18.500
Ideally, we'd like to know,

00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:22.440
if we infected a monkey
three or four years ago

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:25.270
or five or 10 or 15 or 20 years ago,

00:37:25.270 --> 00:37:29.110
and we re expose it to the
same virus, does it get sick?

00:37:29.110 --> 00:37:31.260
We'd like to do this
experiment with a time gap

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:34.520
not a four weeks, but of
four years or four decades

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:37.190
to understand how long
the immunity is lasting,

00:37:37.190 --> 00:37:39.510
how long is that memory lasting?

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:41.400
But of course, that's not possible

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:43.160
with a virus like COVID-19,

00:37:43.160 --> 00:37:45.510
which has emerged in the past six months.

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:48.330
That we didn't know that this
virus existed five years ago,

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:50.540
we couldn't have been infecting monkeys

00:37:50.540 --> 00:37:52.040
to do these experiments five years ago

00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:54.910
because we didn't know
that the virus existed.

00:37:54.910 --> 00:37:58.023
So we're severely limited in
our ability to look backwards,

00:37:59.030 --> 00:38:00.620
to enable us to do these experiments

00:38:00.620 --> 00:38:03.580
to address how long immunity really lasts.

00:38:03.580 --> 00:38:06.730
But we do have an important
data point from this experiment,

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:08.880
which suggests that at the very least,

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:10.580
your immune system is capable

00:38:10.580 --> 00:38:11.850
or the monkey's immune system,

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:13.278
which is extremely similar to yours,

00:38:13.278 --> 00:38:17.943
is capable of remembering
an infection, full stop.

00:38:19.510 --> 00:38:21.530
And then there's a second question

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:25.520
to do with whether immunity
is gonna last in its entirety

00:38:25.520 --> 00:38:28.350
or whether it might be
remembered in some degree.

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:30.930
So even if immunity expires fully,

00:38:30.930 --> 00:38:32.920
are you protected by some degree,

00:38:32.920 --> 00:38:35.230
by the memory that you have?

00:38:35.230 --> 00:38:37.750
So even if you get sick the second time,

00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:39.890
is it possible that you
get sick less severely

00:38:39.890 --> 00:38:42.300
than you got sick the first
time you got infected?

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:44.520
Because these memories are not a binary,

00:38:44.520 --> 00:38:46.153
yes, you remembered, no
you didn't remember thing,

00:38:46.153 --> 00:38:47.683
there's a continuum.

00:38:49.267 --> 00:38:50.730
And then of course we need to think

00:38:50.730 --> 00:38:53.186
about the public health
considerations of this.

00:38:53.186 --> 00:38:54.767
If we do develop a vaccine,

00:38:54.767 --> 00:38:58.590
are people gonna need regular
treatment with this vaccine

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:01.350
or are they gonna be able
to be vaccinated just once?

00:39:01.350 --> 00:39:03.820
And can we use a measure
like antibody levels

00:39:03.820 --> 00:39:06.883
to predict whether someone
needs to be vaccinated or not?

00:39:08.300 --> 00:39:09.133
Cool.

00:39:09.133 --> 00:39:11.280
So if you weren't listening
to anything I just said

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:15.140
over the last 40 minutes, what's
the answer to the question,

00:39:15.140 --> 00:39:16.960
can I get COVID-19 twice?

00:39:16.960 --> 00:39:19.190
Well, what I would say is
this, in the short term

00:39:19.190 --> 00:39:21.060
it's extremely unlikely.

00:39:21.060 --> 00:39:23.900
We've seen that from the
monkey over a period of months,

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:26.040
it's unlikely that your immune
system is gonna forget it,

00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:28.893
the monkey's immune
system certainly did not.

00:39:28.893 --> 00:39:32.680
But, we're not sure about
when immunity might expire

00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:34.870
in the medium term or the long term

00:39:34.870 --> 00:39:37.350
because we haven't been able
to do this experiments yet.

00:39:37.350 --> 00:39:39.320
We we didn't know this virus existed

00:39:39.320 --> 00:39:41.330
more than six months ago.

00:39:41.330 --> 00:39:43.530
And if we look to the best evidence

00:39:43.530 --> 00:39:45.730
that we can speculate with,

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:47.260
it would come from looking at viruses,

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:50.260
which are similar to SARS-CoV-2

00:39:50.260 --> 00:39:51.950
like the original SARS virus

00:39:51.950 --> 00:39:54.610
and MERS, middle East
Respiratory Syndrome.

00:39:54.610 --> 00:39:57.260
If we look at those
viruses, we have some data.

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:01.230
It's not foolproof and
it's not a comprehensive,

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:02.676
but we have some data suggesting

00:40:02.676 --> 00:40:06.080
that immunity lasts
around two to four years

00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:08.210
for these related corona viruses.

00:40:08.210 --> 00:40:12.380
So it wouldn't surprise me if
it was a similar time length

00:40:12.380 --> 00:40:14.540
with this virus, but we don't know.

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:16.260
It is different, it's a different virus

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.340
and it could be longer,
it could be shorter.

00:40:18.340 --> 00:40:20.580
That remains for scientists to establish.

00:40:20.580 --> 00:40:22.850
Rest assured, there's lots
of people pretty hard at work

00:40:22.850 --> 00:40:24.330
on this problem.

00:40:24.330 --> 00:40:25.163
So that's it from me.

00:40:25.163 --> 00:40:26.570
I'd just like to say a big thank you

00:40:26.570 --> 00:40:28.339
to the whole of the D4P team

00:40:28.339 --> 00:40:31.320
from all over the world and the US,

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:34.290
and invite questions from any of you guys

00:40:36.320 --> 00:40:38.450
if my talk has formed them.

00:40:38.450 --> 00:40:39.600
So thank you very much.

